{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, an original fictional passage of about 250 words that comes immediately AFTER the current text in the same invented story.", "text": "Mara knew it at once, even from below: the chipped blue rim, the thin crack that ran down one side like a vein. Their grandmother's cup. It had gone into the river with her the night of the flood, along with the kettle, the table, and half the kitchen wall. They had found the first cup tangled in the reeds a week later. They had never found the second. \"That's not possible,\" Tobias whispered. The heron stepped aside, and the door at the base of the mill swung inward on its own. Warm air rolled out, smelling of flour and woodsmoke and something sweeter underneath, like the anise biscuits Gran used to bake on Sundays. The wheel groaned, turning backward, and with every turn the light in the stone pulsed in time. Mara looked at her brother. His face was pale, but his grip on her sleeve had not loosened. \"Together,\" she said. \"Together,\" he answered. They crossed the threshold. The door closed behind them with a soft, final click, and the sound of the river vanished entirely. The inside of the mill was not ruined. The floor was swept, the millstones gleamed, and a narrow stair wound upward toward the lantern glow. On the bottom step sat a folded square of paper, pinned beneath a smooth grey pebble. Tobias picked it up with trembling fingers and read it aloud: \"You're late. The tea's gone cold, but I'll put the kettle back on. Mind the seventh step.\" It was signed with a single letter. E."}
